# Developing the book and program together

In an ordinary codebase, editing a source file and editing its design document are
two separate acts. In Literate they are one act: authoritative chapters contain the
explanation and the fragments from which much of the program is generated. A good
contributor workflow must therefore protect two products at once—the program that
runs and the book that explains why it has that shape.

The central discipline is simple:

> Change the authoritative work, regenerate its derived artifacts, and review both
> meanings before accepting either.

This chapter gives a durable method for doing that. Exact tool releases and host
installation details belong in the package manifest and contributor reference;
the method remains useful as those details change.

## Know which artifact has authority

Not every repository file has the same role. Authoritative `.literate` chapters own
the generated implementation fragments and their rendered HTML. Generated source
is checked in so a fresh checkout can bootstrap and ordinary language tools can
work with it, but it is not the place to make lasting implementation edits.

Other files may remain ordinary authoritative sources: package metadata, styles,
templates, fixtures, licenses, build configuration, and tests not generated from a
chapter. Some tests are themselves generated, so a contributor must determine the
owner of the particular test before editing it. “The book is the source” does not
mean that every byte in the repository must be hidden in a fragment. It means that
code represented by the book must be changed there rather than patched in its
derived file.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Intent[Design or behavior change] --> Authority{Which source owns it?}
    Authority -->|Explained generated code| Chapter[Edit prose and fragments]
    Authority -->|Ordinary source not generated from a chapter| Ordinary[Edit ordinary source]
    Chapter --> Generate[Regenerate derived source and HTML]
    Ordinary --> Review[Review direct changes]
    Generate --> Review
    Review --> Verify[Verify behavior, explanation, and artifacts]
```

The branches identify ownership, not importance. An ordinary test can be as
critical as a generated engine module. The distinction prevents a successful local
patch from being erased by the next generation.

## Begin with an invariant, not a file

Before editing, state the change in terms a reader and reviewer can test. Examples
include:

- every generated destination is validated before any write;
- a language-server edit may affect only one exact source occurrence;
- the screen and print renderers resolve the same logical annotation;
- a prose-only reorganization leaves generated program bytes unchanged.

An invariant provides a better search key than a filename. Use inspection,
fragment navigation, references, and the assembly maps to find the fragments and
ordinary files that realize it. Read the surrounding explanation before changing
an implementation detail; the prose may reveal constraints that the generated
source no longer makes obvious.

This approach also improves the chapter. If the intended change cannot be explained
without naming a private helper, the design may not yet be stated at the right
level.

## Edit explanation and implementation as one unit

A durable chapter normally introduces the human problem, gives a conceptual model,
states its safety or correctness boundary, and only then presents the selected
implementation. When behavior changes, revise every part whose truth changes:

- motivation, when the supported use or tradeoff changes;
- diagrams, when ownership or sequence changes;
- prose contracts, when accepted inputs or failures change;
- fragments, when executable behavior changes;
- tests and fixtures, when observable outcomes change;
- reference material, when commands or supported environments change.

Do not add prose that merely translates each statement into English. Explain why
the statements belong together, what must remain true, and what is deliberately
not supported. Conversely, do not leave an important invariant only in prose if a
test can make its failure visible.

Fragment boundaries deserve the same review as function boundaries. A fragment
should express one explainable responsibility, have a name that reads naturally in
the surrounding sentence, and compose with its siblings into a useful outline.
Mechanical imports, gathering roots, and routine registration belong in the
assembly or integration references when they would interrupt the main argument.

## Regeneration is a semantic comparison

Generation is not clerical cleanup at the end of a change. It is the operation that
reveals what the authoritative edit means to compilers, browsers, and readers.
After regeneration, classify every changed artifact.

A useful review matrix has three common cases:

- **Prose-only change:** rendered chapters should change; generated program source
  should not.
- **Behavioral fragment change:** the expected generated modules and tests should
  change; unrelated generated files should not.
- **Structural chapter change:** generated program bytes may remain equal while
  document order, anchors, indexes, or publication pages change.

Unexpected absence matters as much as unexpected presence. If a fragment edit
produces no source diff, the contributor may have edited an unused family or the
wrong contribution. If moving a chapter changes source bytes, contribution order
may carry more semantics than the edit intended.

The ordinary loop is concise:

```sh
yarn literate generate
yarn literate check
yarn test
yarn esbuild
```

The repository's contributor reference remains authoritative for available scripts
and prerequisites. The important sequence is generate, compare, test, and build—not
the permanence of one wrapper command.

## Use a validation ladder

No single command answers every question. Validation should move from the smallest
relevant claim to broader integration.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Source[Authoritative edit] --> Inspect[Inspect fragments and planned outputs]
    Inspect --> Focused[Run focused parser or subsystem tests]
    Focused --> Generated[Check generated files are current]
    Generated --> Self[Verify the generation fixed point when relevant]
    Self --> Suite[Run the complete automated suite]
    Suite --> Package[Build the shipped entry points]
    Package --> Real[Exercise real hosts or external tools where needed]
```

Each arrow adds evidence of a different kind:

1. **Inspection** checks that definitions, references, roots, and destinations are
   the ones intended.
2. **Focused tests** give fast feedback near the changed policy.
3. **Generated-file checks** compare the complete in-memory plan with tracked
   artifacts without treating stale output as source.
4. **Self-host verification** type-checks fresh generated sources and asks the fresh
   engine to reproduce the plan.
5. **The full suite** catches interactions outside the contributor's first model.
6. **Packaging builds** prove that actual extension and runtime entry points can be
   assembled, not merely imported by tests.
7. **Real-host acceptance** covers behavior that mocks cannot establish, such as
   editor UI, a language server, a browser, or a typesetter.

Not every change needs every expensive final step. The choice should follow the
claim. A typography change needs rendered screen and print inspection; a protocol
change needs framing and real-server evidence; an engine change needs the fixed
point. Record skipped environmental checks honestly rather than implying that a
headless suite performed them.

## Changing the generator requires a bridge

Self-hosting creates a bootstrapping problem: the old generated engine must first
understand enough of the edited book to produce the new engine. A safe generator
change therefore has two conceptual generations.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant B as Authoritative book
    participant O as Known-working engine
    participant N as Fresh engine
    participant V as Verification

    B->>O: Generate candidate outputs
    O-->>N: Candidate engine source
    N->>V: Type-check and build
    V->>N: Generate from the same book
    N-->>V: Candidate outputs again
    V->>V: Compare paths and bytes
```

If the first candidate does not build, repair the literate source and use a
known-working bootstrap executable again. Do not hand-edit generated TypeScript as
a second source of truth. A syntax migration may need a compatibility bridge so the
old parser can generate the new parser before the book adopts the new form.

The fixed-point check is intentionally narrower than the test suite. It proves that
the generated engine agrees with its own authoritative input. It does not prove
that command-line effects, editor activation, or publication tools behave correctly.
Those remain separate layers in the validation ladder.

## Review changes in both reading orders

A literate program has at least two meaningful orders:

- the chapter order in which a person encounters ideas;
- the assembled order in which generated source reaches language tools.

Review the chapter while skipping code blocks. The argument should remain coherent,
technical terms should be introduced before use, and diagrams should answer a
specific question. Then read parent fragments as outlines and inspect the generated
diff. The assembly should remain complete, and fragment names should explain why
pieces are connected.

This double reading catches opposite failures. A program can compile while its
book has become a change log or a source dump. A chapter can read beautifully while
its fragments assemble an unintended program. Neither is a successful literate
change.

## Treat generated files as review evidence

Checked-in generated files serve practical purposes: bootstrapping, ordinary
language tooling, packaging, and transparent review of what the book produces.
They should be committed with the authoritative chapters that changed them.

Review them without granting them authority. Useful questions include:

- Are changed paths exactly those predicted by the edited roots?
- Is the diff a semantic change or only ordering and whitespace?
- Did a prose edit accidentally enter generated source?
- Did a moved contribution change assembly order?
- Are generated HTML links and identities still valid?
- Did generation leave an old orphan that requires deliberate removal?

Generation does not delete obsolete destinations automatically. Renaming or
removing a root therefore requires an explicit inventory review; an old file can
remain plausible long after it has stopped belonging to the plan.

## Package what users will actually run

Tests often import modules directly, while users run bundled entry points with
packaged assets. Before release, build those entry points and inspect the package
contents. Confirm that runtime bundles, browser programs, templates, styles,
licenses, and declared contributions are present, and that development-only or
private material is absent.

External publication tools and language servers deserve separate acceptance. A
mock can prove how Literate reacts to a response; it cannot prove compatibility
with every real executable. Test representative supported configurations, record
the boundary of that evidence, and keep missing-tool diagnostics actionable. The
program should not download compilers, browsers, fonts, or servers merely to make
a release check pass.

## Version and release the whole contract

A release version describes more than executable bytes. It also describes syntax,
commands, settings, generated-file expectations, publication formats, packaged
resources, and known limitations. Release review should therefore include:

1. update the version and user-facing change record consistently;
2. regenerate and require a clean generated-file check;
3. run the relevant fixed-point, test, lint, and package builds;
4. inspect the distributable archive rather than assuming its manifest is enough;
5. exercise installation or host acceptance for changed integration surfaces;
6. review third-party notices and portable asset licenses;
7. verify that examples and contributor instructions match the shipped behavior;
8. publish only the reviewed artifact, preserving a traceable source state.

A release command can automate these steps, but it cannot decide whether the book
is clear, a diagram is legible, or a PDF has an awkward page break. Human review is
part of the release contract for a program whose documentation is itself a primary
product.

## Keep the loop sustainable

The method works only if contributors can run it repeatedly. Keep focused tests
fast, make diagnostics point back to authoritative chapters, preserve a dependable
bootstrap, and avoid requiring a graphical editor for core generation. Expensive
real-tool checks can be explicit without being forgotten; package scripts and
contributor references should make their purpose clear.

Most of all, keep the claims proportional to the evidence. “The fixed point passed”
is not “the extension is correct.” “The headless suite passed” is not “every
language server is compatible.” “The PDF compiled” is not “the typography needs no
review.” This precision makes both maintenance and release more trustworthy.

The method keeps book and program aligned, but it cannot remove every cost of
literate programming or every limitation of the surrounding tools. The next
chapter states those boundaries directly.
